#---
# purpose: "a list that a continuation captures is not made shared by the capture: the continuation's one-cons rewrite of it runs in place, and only a call that receives an alias of the same cell refuses the rewrite"
#---
#
# `Enum.count` is a call with a continuation, and the continuation holds the
# cells the code after the call still names.
#
# In the first case the continuation holds `xs` while the call receives an
# unrelated list. Nothing else names the cell, so `[h | [n]]` rewrites it in
# place: list retention telemetry counts a hit. If building the continuation
# marked its captured lists shared, this rewrite would always allocate.
#
# In the second case the call receives `src` while the continuation holds
# `twin`, which is the very same cell. The compiler's call-site ownership rule
# (`construct_call_ownership` in src/compiler2/jobs/body.rs) publishes the call
# argument because the continuation retains an alias of it, so the later rewrite
# finds the cell shared and allocates a fresh one: a retention attempt without a
# hit. `src` is still `[4, 5, 6]` afterwards.
#
# Across the run: three retention attempts, two hits. `twin = [ah | at]` is the
# third attempt: it rebuilds the cell it was taken from with identical contents,
# so it keeps that cell. The missing hit is the refused rewrite of the shared cell.
def main() do
  xs = Enum.to_list(1..3)
  n = Enum.count([0, 0])
  [h | _] = xs
  dbg([h | [n]])

  src = Enum.to_list(4..6)
  [ah | at] = src
  twin = [ah | at]
  m = Enum.count(src)
  [bh | _] = twin
  dbg([bh | [m]])
  dbg(src)
end
